1
00:00:00,056 --> 00:00:04,861
Lakehouse is A data architecture that combines the cheap, flexible storage of a

2
00:00:04,861 --> 00:00:09,847
data lake with the reliability and performance of a data warehouse.

3
00:00:09,847 --> 00:00:16,083
All your data sits in one place and can be used for both BI/SQL and AI/ML

4
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:17,861
workloads.

5
00:00:17,861 --> 00:00:23,694
Lakehouse can be depickted as a library with books Cloud storage is The object

6
00:00:23,694 --> 00:00:29,833
storage where the data files physically live, such as AWS S3, Azure Data Lake

7
00:00:29,833 --> 00:00:34,361
Storage (ADLS) or Google Cloud Storage.

8
00:00:34,361 --> 00:00:37,569
Databricks doesn’t own this storage.

9
00:00:37,569 --> 00:00:41,291
It reads and writes files in your cloud account.

10
00:00:41,291 --> 00:00:45,417
Cloud storage can be presented as shelves with books.

11
00:00:45,417 --> 00:00:51,889
The shelves are rented from a cloud provider (AWS, Azure, GCP) and aren’t part of

12
00:00:51,889 --> 00:00:54,069
Databricks itself.

13
00:00:54,069 --> 00:00:59,597
Delta Lake format is An open table format that stores data as Parquet files plus a

14
00:00:59,597 --> 00:01:03,958
transaction log (_delta_log).

15
00:01:03,958 --> 00:01:09,708
The log adds ACID transactions, schema enforcement, versioning (“time travel”) and

16
00:01:09,708 --> 00:01:14,486
safe updates, deletes and merges on top of plain files.

17
00:01:14,486 --> 00:01:19,278
Each library book is a table and Delta Lake is the way it’s written.

18
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:24,902
A Delta “book” also has a change history at the back (the transaction log).

19
00:01:24,902 --> 00:01:29,093
That’s what makes time travel and safe edits possible.

20
00:01:29,149 --> 00:01:35,482
Unity Catalog is Databricks’ central governance layer for data and AI assets.

21
00:01:35,482 --> 00:01:40,579
It keeps the metadata (catalog, then schema , then table).

22
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:45,593
it controls who can access what, and records lineage and audit logs across

23
00:01:45,593 --> 00:01:47,538
workspaces.

24
00:01:47,538 --> 00:01:52,454
Unity Catalog can be depicted as a computer system with a librarian: and it’s a

25
00:01:52,454 --> 00:01:55,496
little more than a catalog of books.

26
00:01:55,496 --> 00:02:00,634
The librarian also decides who may borrow which book (permissions), keeps a record

27
00:02:00,634 --> 00:02:06,149
of who read what (auditing) and tracks where books came from (lineage).

28
00:02:06,149 --> 00:02:10,510
The people who come to read and work with the books are the Databricks compute (

29
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:13,593
clusters and SQL warehouses).

30
00:02:13,593 --> 00:02:18,240
The library stays the same no matter how many readers come in.

